[Music]  welcome back to cbs mornings almost all  of the electronics we use rely on  microchips the demand for them jumped  during the pandemic and production  disruptions and supply chain issues made  it difficult for suppliers to keep up  chip shortages cost the economy billions  that's right billions in 2021. now  experts want the government to step in  and help american manufacturers make  more chips meg oliver is in malta new  york 20 miles north of albany inside one  of the factories at the forefront of  chip production in the u.s meg that's a  serious suit and a serious operation  over there isn't it posing  you need a little attitude to wear this  nate i gotta tell you good morning no  one is allowed in here unless you're  dressed like me from head to toe that's  because we're in the clean room where  they like to boast it's a thousand times  more sanitary than an operating room  they produce about a million microchips  daily but the need is so great i need to  put out at least 10 times more than that  how big is this facility so this  facility is about 420 000 square feet  and that's a  give or take about seven football fields  here at global foundries in upstate new  york everything looks yellow special  lighting is used to protect the delicate  production of microchips engineer  christopher belfy is equipment manager  how complex is it to make these  microchips they're in everything we use  phones tvs cars  it is extremely complex and as the  demand on capability increases so does  the complexity chips are needed for just  about all devices laptops home  appliances like refrigerators gaming  consoles and medical equipment the chips  are the brains of devices the u.s  produces just around 12 percent of the  world's supply and we've relied heavily  on ships produced in asia but when the  pandemic hit it shut down major  production plans decreasing supply we  don't want to rely on foreign entities  to be able to give us the brains and the  guts of what drive our everyday  vehicles devices our cell phones there  is renewed focus on producing more chips  at home as some experts warn not doing  so could also be a national security  risk the house is currently considering  the chips for america act that would  provide u.s manufacturers with 52  billion dollars in funding to help boost  domestic production why is it so  important to manufacture on american  soil well i think it starts first and  foremost  with security it's kind of trusted  supply chain  the second is just think about the  economic activity a manufacturing  facility brings to the u.s we employ  over 3000 employees at this site tom  caulfield is the ceo of global foundries  in addition to upstate new york they  have facilities in vermont germany and  singapore what stage are we at in this  chip shortage i'd like to be more  optimistic and tell you that right  around the corner helps on its way  just the complexity of technology that  it takes years to put capacity on and i  think  for the better part of the next five to  ten years  we'll be chasing supply in this industry  not demand experts estimate the chip  shortage cost the u.s economy around 240  billion dollars last year a recent  report from the department of commerce  found in 2021 demand for chips were as  high as 17 percent more than in 2019.  this is what everybody wants this is the  gold caulfield says they'll continue to  try to meet demand but they need private  investments and federal funding through  the chips bill it's really important  that we have supply chain security in  the u.s and know and sleep well at night  that gf will do their part  when the chips bill is funded  both the house and senate have passed  their own versions of the chips act and  while there appears to be  bipartisan agreement there is not an  official agreement just yet so no  funding tony  yeah people want that supply chain to be  secure we used to make a lot of chips  good to see that we are ramping up  production once again meg oliver in the  suit in malta new york thank you very  much

